<article class="env-definition" id="027R">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/027R">Definition <span data-tag="027R">111.22.1</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. Recall that a <em>finite locally free</em> $A$-module $M$ is a module such that for every ${\mathfrak p} \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$ there exists an $f\in A$, $f \not\in {\mathfrak p}$ such that $M_ f$ is a finite free $A_ f$-module. We say $M$ is an <em>invertible module</em> if $M$ is finite locally free of rank $1$, i.e., for every ${\mathfrak p} \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$ there exists an $f\in A$, $f \not\in \mathfrak p$ such that $M_ f \cong A_ f$ as an $A_ f$-module. </p>
</article>